+\begin{theorem}[Native small-regime global polynomial pair]
+\label{thm:qld-native-small-regime-global-pair}
+\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.quantitative_native_global_pair_error_bound}
+\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.exists_quantitative_global_pair_witness_at_native_error}
+\leanok
+\uses{thm:qld-native-rounded-polynomials,
+  thm:qld-coefficient-thirty-rounding-scalars}
+Let $P=(q,m,d)$ be admissible, $n=md$, and $\rho=n/q$.  If
+$0\leq e\leq1$ and $\rho\leq1$, take
+\[
+ Q=E_{\rm pt}(e),\qquad
+ L_{\rm ext}=mE_{\rm ext}(e,\rho),\qquad
+ \lambda=\Lambda_{P_{\rm ext}}
+   \bigl(3(\sqrt{Q+L_{\rm ext}}+\rho)\bigr),
+\]
+and set
+\[
+ G=\min\!\left\{1,
+ 32\eta(\lambda)+64Q+\frac{12n+4d+14}{q}\right\}.
+\]
+There are complete projective global polynomial-pair measurements whose four
+point defects are bounded by this concrete $G$.  The same witness also obeys
+the common-envelope consequence
+\[
+ G\leq10^7n^4E_{\rm pair}(P,e).
+\]
+\end{theorem}
+\begin{proof}
+\leanok
+\uses{thm:qld-native-rounded-polynomials,
+  thm:qld-coefficient-thirty-rounding-scalars}
+Construct the point and extended-line witnesses, apply the native pair
+constructor, and cap each defect by one.  Monotonicity of the separated-power
+rounding error and the native-to-coefficient-$30$ comparison give the final
+common-envelope inequality for these same measurements.
+\end{proof}
